# Compose variables for the PhotoTools server.
#
# With deploy/deploy.sh this file is OPTIONAL. The script takes the Firebase and
# Google values from the repository-root .env, the paths and the port from
# deploy/deploy.env, and uses this file only for what must differ on the NAS —
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI above all, since the Mac's points at localhost.
# Anything set here wins; the paths and PORT are ignored, as deploy.env owns
# them.
#
# Running compose by hand, copy to `deploy/.env` — beside docker-compose.yml,
# NOT at the repository root, which is a different file compose never reads.
#
# These are the *host* paths and the identifiers. They are not the container's
# environment: docker-compose.yml maps each one to a mount point and sets the
# variables the server actually reads. No secret belongs in the repository
# (spec §9.2 rule 4), so nothing here is filled in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required — compose refuses to start without all five
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The photo library on the NAS. Mounted at /library, and ROOTS is set to exactly
# that, so no request can reach anything else (G6).
LIBRARY_PATH=/volume1/photo

# The ledger and the staging directory. A different volume from the library on
# purpose: §7 wants the database outside the photographs, so losing one cannot
# corrupt the other.
DATA_PATH=/volume1/docker/phototools

# The folder publishing draws from and, on success, EMPTIES of everything Google
# confirmed receiving.
#
# Not the library, and not a folder containing it — the server refuses to start
# if it is either. Copy photographs in, review the dry run, publish; what Google
# confirms is then deleted from here and nowhere else.
PUBLISHING_PATH=/volume1/docker/phototools/publishing

# Firebase. ALLOWED_UIDS is the only thing restricting access to the library:
# Firebase will authenticate any Google account in existence, so an empty list
# means nobody rather than everybody (§5.3).
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
ALLOWED_UIDS=aBcDeFgHiJkLmNoPqRsTuVwXyZ12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host port. The container always listens on 3000.
# PORT=3000

# Image tag, if you are pulling a built image rather than building here.
# PHOTOTOOLS_TAG=latest

# Break-glass token for when Firebase is unreachable (§5.3). Empty means
# unavailable, not "permit anything".
# ADMIN_TOKEN=

# F12 thresholds. Defaults match the code: no resolution ceiling, 10 MB, 90 days.
# MAX_MEGAPIXELS=0
# MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES=10485760
# MAX_AGE_DAYS=90

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Photos (F15) — read when publishing is used, not at startup, so a
# server with none of these set still starts and serves everything else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# From a **Web application** OAuth client. The redirect URI must match what is
# registered on that client character for character, scheme and port included.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://nas.local:3000/api/connectors/google/callback

# 32 bytes, hex: openssl rand -hex 32
# Connecting refuses outright without this rather than storing the refresh token
# in the clear.
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# RUST_LOG=phototools_server=info,tower_http=info
